1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:03,420
Correlation helps compare candidate placements.

2
00:00:03,882 --> 00:00:09,007
Its interpretation depends on overlap, preprocessing and search range.

3
00:00:09,461 --> 00:00:13,336
One high coefficient proves neither the date nor its cause.

4
00:00:13,789 --> 00:00:19,316
Statistics help identify candidates, but short overlaps and few distinctive features can

5
00:00:19,329 --> 00:00:21,648
produce chance matches.

6
00:00:22,451 --> 00:00:26,787
Short overlaps provide fewer ways to reject chance matches.

7
00:00:27,243 --> 00:00:32,591
Required length depends on pattern, species, region and other evidence.

8
00:00:33,045 --> 00:00:37,473
A 30-ring exercise does not establish a 30-ring dating rule.

9
00:00:37,927 --> 00:00:43,111
The same correlation over different overlap lengths represents different evidence.

10
00:00:43,572 --> 00:00:48,508
Inspect duration and the distribution of distinctive features together.

11
00:00:49,311 --> 00:00:51,430
Re-examine the evidence.

12
00:00:51,892 --> 00:00:57,050
Check surfaces, images, boundaries, measurements and transcription.

13
00:00:57,511 --> 00:00:59,695
Assess reference suitability.

14
00:01:00,155 --> 00:01:05,438
Check species, region, period and whether shared variation is expected.

15
00:01:05,892 --> 00:01:08,241
Retain an unresolved result.

16
00:01:08,695 --> 00:01:12,432
Do not force calendar dates when evidence is insufficient.

17
00:01:12,892 --> 00:01:16,149
Do not force an unmatched sample into a date.

18
00:01:16,603 --> 00:01:21,656
Review boundaries, species, region and reference applicability.

19
00:01:22,459 --> 00:01:26,677
Sample ID, sampling position and observed boundaries.

20
00:01:27,132 --> 00:01:30,738
Reference and version, candidate dates and overlap.

21
00:01:31,198 --> 00:01:34,705
Matching and mismatching features, with rechecks.

22
00:01:35,159 --> 00:01:38,692
Handling of ring anomalies, analyst and date.

23
00:01:39,145 --> 00:01:44,974
Retain candidates, mismatches and reinspection locations so another reader can evaluate the

24
00:01:44,988 --> 00:01:47,148
dating decision later.

25
00:01:47,951 --> 00:01:50,570
Shared variation supports matching.

26
00:01:51,032 --> 00:01:54,947
Growth also contains individual and disturbance effects.

27
00:01:55,401 --> 00:01:58,190
Test ring dates with multiple features.

28
00:01:58,651 --> 00:02:02,131
Observed ring counts can differ from the calendar span.

29
00:02:02,585 --> 00:02:05,611
Replication and independent checks matter.

30
00:02:06,072 --> 00:02:09,611
Investigate mismatches as well as apparent agreement.

31
00:02:10,072 --> 00:02:16,190
Dating combines visual inspection, statistical support, replication and calendar anchoring,

32
00:02:16,361 --> 00:02:19,432
with none of those steps standing alone.

33
00:02:20,235 --> 00:02:22,782
Resources and further practice.

34
00:02:23,237 --> 00:02:28,014
Use the linked exercises to practise the procedure on series of different lengths and

35
00:02:28,027 --> 00:02:30,308
preservation states.

36
00:02:31,111 --> 00:02:31,709
1.

37
00:02:31,847 --> 00:02:34,757
How does a missing ring affect a simple count?.

38
00:02:35,061 --> 00:02:35,554
2.

39
00:02:35,626 --> 00:02:39,912
Is agreement with a mean containing the unknown an independent test?.

40
00:02:40,219 --> 00:02:43,146
Write one sentence explaining each answer.

41
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:48,942
Alongside your answer, identify further evidence that could strengthen or change the dating

42
00:02:48,955 --> 00:02:50,472
decision.

43
00:02:51,275 --> 00:02:54,566
Which sample and measurement radius should be used?.

44
00:02:54,869 --> 00:02:58,764
Measure widths, preserve records and verify dating.

45
00:02:59,225 --> 00:03:06,751
Preparation: BG01 mathematics/statistics and glossary T32–T38.

46
00:03:07,211 --> 00:03:11,593
Next we move from sampling through measurement to chronology construction.

47
00:03:12,054 --> 00:03:16,564
Traceable records preserve the evidence throughout that process.
